1
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:07,080
Four hundred kilometers above your head,

2
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:12,552
a small metal room is falling around the Earth at twenty-eight thousand kilometers

3
00:00:12,592 --> 00:00:13,343
per hour.

4
00:00:14,443 --> 00:00:18,116
Inside it, the lights are dim. The air tastes like metal.

5
00:00:18,156 --> 00:00:22,932
And every breath you take leaves a little less oxygen for the next one.

6
00:00:24,032 --> 00:00:25,205
The radio is dead.

7
00:00:25,245 --> 00:00:28,910
The ship that was supposed to bring you home is broken.

8
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:33,087
And below you, eight billion people are going about their day,

9
00:00:33,127 --> 00:00:35,568
with no idea that you are up here.

10
00:00:36,668 --> 00:00:38,564
This is the story of Noah.

11
00:00:38,604 --> 00:00:42,477
And of the eleven days he spent stranded in space.

12
00:00:52,077 --> 00:00:57,560
After the Sahara, Noah swore he would stay home for a while. No more deserts.

13
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,260
No more oceans. No more adventures.

14
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:04,323
That lasted exactly four months.

15
00:01:05,423 --> 00:01:08,294
The email came from a private space company.

16
00:01:08,334 --> 00:01:13,125
They were sending a short mission to their new orbital station, Meridian.

17
00:01:13,165 --> 00:01:16,698
And for the first time, they wanted to send an artist.

18
00:01:16,738 --> 00:01:18,815
Someone to photograph the Earth,

19
00:01:18,855 --> 00:01:22,975
and tell ordinary people what it really feels like up there.

20
00:01:24,075 --> 00:01:29,446
They had read about the bunker, the Arctic, the Amazon, the ocean and the desert.

21
00:01:29,486 --> 00:01:31,116
Their message was simple.

22
00:01:31,156 --> 00:01:34,980
If anyone can stay calm when things go wrong, it's you.

23
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:38,819
His commander would be Vera Lindqvist.

24
00:01:38,859 --> 00:01:41,964
Fifty-four years old, from northern Sweden.

25
00:01:42,004 --> 00:01:45,694
Four missions, more than six hundred days in orbit.

26
00:01:45,734 --> 00:01:51,690
A woman who had once fixed a broken toilet in zero gravity with a spoon and a hair

27
00:01:51,730 --> 00:01:52,173
tie.

28
00:01:53,273 --> 00:01:56,791
Up there, Noah, there is no such thing as a small problem.

29
00:01:56,831 --> 00:01:58,386
A loose screw is a bullet.

30
00:01:58,426 --> 00:02:00,410
A drop of water is a fire hazard.

31
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:04,274
And a mistake you make on Monday tries to kill you on Thursday.

32
00:02:04,314 --> 00:02:06,795
So we don't make mistakes. Understood?

33
00:02:07,895 --> 00:02:09,913
For six months, Noah trained.

34
00:02:09,953 --> 00:02:13,036
He spun in centrifuges until he blacked out.

35
00:02:13,076 --> 00:02:16,017
He floated in a giant pool in a spacesuit,

36
00:02:16,057 --> 00:02:21,814
practicing for a spacewalk he was told he would never have to do. Just in case.

37
00:02:22,914 --> 00:02:23,928
Vera, be honest.

38
00:02:23,968 --> 00:02:26,488
Am I ever really going to need this?

39
00:02:27,588 --> 00:02:30,125
I hope not. That's why we practice it.

40
00:02:35,225 --> 00:02:38,874
The launch was scheduled for a cold morning in March.

41
00:02:38,914 --> 00:02:42,702
At dawn, the rocket stood on the pad, white and silent,

42
00:02:42,742 --> 00:02:46,789
breathing clouds of frozen vapor like a sleeping animal.

43
00:02:47,889 --> 00:02:50,430
Noah lay on his back in the capsule,

44
00:02:50,470 --> 00:02:54,661
strapped into a seat molded to the exact shape of his body.

45
00:02:54,701 --> 00:02:57,242
Above him, seventy meters of rocket.

46
00:02:57,282 --> 00:03:00,085
Below him, five hundred tons of fuel.

47
00:03:01,185 --> 00:03:04,443
Ember, Control. All systems are go. Ten, nine, eight...

48
00:03:08,543 --> 00:03:12,513
The engines ignited with a roar that Noah felt in his teeth.

49
00:03:12,553 --> 00:03:16,190
The capsule shook so hard he couldn't read the screens.

50
00:03:16,230 --> 00:03:20,534
An invisible hand pressed him into his seat, heavier and heavier,

51
00:03:20,574 --> 00:03:22,863
until breathing itself was work.

52
00:03:23,963 --> 00:03:26,943
Eight and a half minutes. That's all it took.

53
00:03:26,983 --> 00:03:31,707
The blue sky outside the window turned purple, then indigo, then black.

54
00:03:31,747 --> 00:03:33,049
The engines stopped.

55
00:03:33,089 --> 00:03:35,588
And suddenly the pressure was gone.

56
00:03:36,688 --> 00:03:41,302
A pen floated up from Noah's pocket and drifted slowly past his face.

57
00:03:41,342 --> 00:03:42,515
He reached for it.

58
00:03:42,555 --> 00:03:46,752
And then he looked out the window. And forgot about the pen.

59
00:03:47,852 --> 00:03:49,913
Oh my God. Vera. Look at it.

60
00:03:51,013 --> 00:03:55,807
The Earth filled the window. Not a map. Not a photograph. A living thing.

61
00:03:55,847 --> 00:04:00,641
Swirling white clouds, deep blue oceans, the brown wrinkles of mountains.

62
00:04:00,681 --> 00:04:06,137
And around all of it, a line of light so thin it looked painted on. The atmosphere.

63
00:04:06,177 --> 00:04:11,294
Everything that keeps every human alive, thinner than the skin of an apple.

64
00:04:16,394 --> 00:04:19,921
Six hours later, the capsule docked with Meridian.

65
00:04:19,961 --> 00:04:24,345
Four connected modules, two long golden wings of solar panels,

66
00:04:24,385 --> 00:04:28,174
and a large round window looking down at the Earth.

67
00:04:29,274 --> 00:04:33,633
For the next five days, Noah lived the strangest life of his own.

68
00:04:33,673 --> 00:04:36,746
He slept in a sleeping bag strapped to a wall.

69
00:04:36,786 --> 00:04:39,249
He drank coffee from a plastic pouch.

70
00:04:39,289 --> 00:04:41,821
When he spilled water, it didn't fall.

71
00:04:41,861 --> 00:04:46,951
It floated in trembling silver spheres that he had to catch with a towel.

72
00:04:48,051 --> 00:04:51,860
The station circled the Earth every ninety-two minutes.

73
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:54,310
Which meant sixteen sunrises a day.

74
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:58,719
Every hour and a half, a line of fire swept across the horizon,

75
00:04:58,759 --> 00:05:02,198
and the whole planet lit up in orange and gold.

76
00:05:03,298 --> 00:05:07,954
Noah spent hours at the big window, which the crew called the cupola.

77
00:05:07,994 --> 00:05:10,541
He photographed hurricanes from above.

78
00:05:10,581 --> 00:05:14,284
Lightning storms flickering over Africa like fireflies.

79
00:05:14,324 --> 00:05:17,891
The green curtains of the aurora dancing over Canada.

80
00:05:17,931 --> 00:05:21,280
And at night, cities shining like spilled gold.

81
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:27,851
You can't see any borders from up here. No countries. No lines. Just one place.

82
00:05:28,951 --> 00:05:30,580
Every astronaut says that.

83
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:34,368
And every astronaut forgets it, a few months after landing.

84
00:05:34,408 --> 00:05:36,997
Try to remember it longer than we did.

85
00:05:38,097 --> 00:05:42,988
On the sixth day, they were supposed to start preparing for the trip home.

86
00:05:43,028 --> 00:05:46,577
Instead, at four in the morning, an alarm went off.

87
00:05:51,677 --> 00:05:52,583
Meridian, Control.

88
00:05:52,623 --> 00:05:54,317
We have an urgent debris warning.

89
00:05:54,357 --> 00:05:58,258
An old weather satellite broke apart two hours ago. Thousands of fragments.

90
00:05:58,298 --> 00:06:00,675
Your orbit crosses the cloud in forty minutes.

91
00:06:00,715 --> 00:06:02,914
Get to the capsule and close the hatch.

92
00:06:04,014 --> 00:06:06,414
Space around the Earth is not empty.

93
00:06:06,454 --> 00:06:10,074
There are more than a million pieces of junk up there.

94
00:06:10,114 --> 00:06:13,531
Dead satellites, old rocket parts, flakes of paint.

95
00:06:13,571 --> 00:06:16,446
Each moving ten times faster than a bullet.

96
00:06:16,486 --> 00:06:21,516
Even a fragment the size of a marble can punch through metal like paper.

97
00:06:22,616 --> 00:06:27,706
Vera and Noah floated into the capsule, sealed the hatch, and strapped in.

98
00:06:27,746 --> 00:06:32,490
If the station was destroyed, the capsule could still take them home.

99
00:06:32,530 --> 00:06:33,928
That was the plan.

100
00:06:35,028 --> 00:06:37,199
Twenty seconds. Hold on to something.

101
00:06:37,239 --> 00:06:40,077
And whatever you hear, don't open that hatch.

102
00:06:41,177 --> 00:06:43,101
At first, there was nothing.

103
00:06:43,141 --> 00:06:45,486
Just silence, and the hum of fans.

104
00:06:45,526 --> 00:06:49,415
Then came a sound like gravel thrown against a tin roof.

105
00:06:49,455 --> 00:06:53,463
Then a bang so loud the whole capsule rang like a bell.

106
00:06:57,063 --> 00:06:58,299
Alarms. Red lights.

107
00:06:58,339 --> 00:07:00,583
A hiss somewhere behind the walls.

108
00:07:00,623 --> 00:07:03,941
The pressure inside the main station was dropping.

109
00:07:03,981 --> 00:07:09,449
Vera opened the hatch and launched herself into the module, Noah right behind her,

110
00:07:09,489 --> 00:07:11,050
hunting for the leak.

111
00:07:12,150 --> 00:07:14,241
They found it in the lab module.

112
00:07:14,281 --> 00:07:19,568
A hole the size of a coin, whistling as the station's air rushed out into space.

113
00:07:19,608 --> 00:07:21,565
There was no time to patch it.

114
00:07:21,605 --> 00:07:24,828
Vera pushed Noah back and slammed the hatch shut.

115
00:07:24,868 --> 00:07:28,414
The heavy door crushed her wrist against the frame.

116
00:07:29,514 --> 00:07:33,578
Agh! It's fine. It's fine. Lock it. Turn the handle, Noah! All the way!

117
00:07:34,678 --> 00:07:37,116
The hissing stopped. They were alive.

118
00:07:37,156 --> 00:07:39,727
But when the alarms finally went quiet,

119
00:07:39,767 --> 00:07:43,210
and Vera started checking the systems with one hand,

120
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:46,815
the silence that followed was worse than any alarm.

121
00:07:51,915 --> 00:07:54,376
The damage report took three hours.

122
00:07:54,416 --> 00:07:57,092
The main antenna had been cut in half.

123
00:07:57,132 --> 00:08:02,809
The backup antenna was on the outside of the lab module, now sealed and airless.

124
00:08:02,849 --> 00:08:05,786
And the oxygen generator was inside it.

125
00:08:06,886 --> 00:08:08,030
Control, Meridian.

126
00:08:08,070 --> 00:08:10,258
Control, Meridian, do you copy?

127
00:08:11,358 --> 00:08:12,697
Nothing. Only static.

128
00:08:12,737 --> 00:08:16,376
For the first time since the beginning of the space age,

129
00:08:16,416 --> 00:08:18,478
nobody on Earth could hear them.

130
00:08:18,518 --> 00:08:21,952
And nobody on Earth knew if they were still alive.

131
00:08:23,052 --> 00:08:25,360
Then Vera went to check the capsule.

132
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:27,969
She pointed a camera at its outer shell,

133
00:08:28,009 --> 00:08:32,398
and stared at the screen for a long time without saying anything.

134
00:08:33,498 --> 00:08:35,984
The heat shield is cracked. A fragment hit it.

135
00:08:36,024 --> 00:08:39,414
If we try to go home in this... we burn up on the way down.

136
00:08:40,514 --> 00:08:43,670
Their ship home was broken. Their radio was dead.

137
00:08:43,710 --> 00:08:46,344
Noah floated in the middle of the module,

138
00:08:46,384 --> 00:08:49,344
looking at the blue planet through the window.

139
00:08:49,384 --> 00:08:52,279
So close he could see the waves on the ocean.

140
00:08:52,319 --> 00:08:54,295
And completely out of reach.

141
00:08:55,395 --> 00:08:58,255
Okay. What do we have? Tell me what we have.

142
00:08:59,355 --> 00:09:03,509
They made a list. Food for a month. Water for three weeks.

143
00:09:03,549 --> 00:09:07,124
Emergency oxygen tanks, enough for maybe six days.

144
00:09:07,164 --> 00:09:11,869
And a problem nobody talks about in the movies. Carbon dioxide.

145
00:09:16,969 --> 00:09:20,732
Every time you breathe out, you release carbon dioxide.

146
00:09:20,772 --> 00:09:23,635
On Earth, plants and wind take care of it.

147
00:09:23,675 --> 00:09:29,097
In a sealed metal room, it builds up. First comes the headache. Then confusion.

148
00:09:29,137 --> 00:09:31,638
Then sleep. And you never wake up.

149
00:09:32,738 --> 00:09:38,039
The station's main air scrubber was in the lab module, behind the sealed hatch.

150
00:09:38,079 --> 00:09:41,960
The capsule had its own, but it was built for short trips.

151
00:09:41,999 --> 00:09:46,071
Its filters were designed to last two days, not two weeks.

152
00:09:47,171 --> 00:09:50,007
By the second day, the headaches had begun.

153
00:09:50,047 --> 00:09:54,220
Noah's thoughts felt slow and sticky, like walking through mud.

154
00:09:54,260 --> 00:09:56,561
Vera's hand was swollen and purple.

155
00:09:56,601 --> 00:10:00,028
She had splinted her wrist with a ruler and tape.

156
00:10:01,128 --> 00:10:03,437
The capsule filters. We can stretch them.

157
00:10:03,477 --> 00:10:07,561
If we pull air through them slower, with more surface, they last longer.

158
00:10:07,601 --> 00:10:10,615
We need a bigger box. Bags, tape, a fan. Anything.

159
00:10:11,715 --> 00:10:12,936
So Noah built one.

160
00:10:12,976 --> 00:10:17,698
Out of storage bags, a hose from the vacuum cleaner, a computer fan,

161
00:10:17,738 --> 00:10:21,340
two spare filters and an entire roll of silver tape.

162
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:25,051
It looked like a child's school project. It was ugly.

163
00:10:25,091 --> 00:10:28,953
And it was the most important thing he had ever made.

164
00:10:30,053 --> 00:10:35,082
When he turned it on, the fan whirred, and the bags slowly puffed up like lungs.

165
00:10:35,122 --> 00:10:39,581
They watched the carbon dioxide meter for an hour, without speaking.

166
00:10:40,681 --> 00:10:42,537
The number stopped rising.

167
00:10:42,577 --> 00:10:45,789
And then, very slowly, it started to fall.

168
00:10:46,889 --> 00:10:49,359
Ha! It's working! Vera, it's working!

169
00:10:50,459 --> 00:10:53,882
It's buying time. Time is good. But time is not a way home.

170
00:10:53,922 --> 00:10:55,950
Someone has to know we're alive.

171
00:11:01,050 --> 00:11:05,038
There was one way to talk to Earth again. The backup antenna.

172
00:11:05,078 --> 00:11:09,133
If someone went outside, unbolted it from the dead lab module,

173
00:11:09,173 --> 00:11:12,633
and connected it to a spare socket on the living one,

174
00:11:12,673 --> 00:11:14,936
they might be able to call home.

175
00:11:16,036 --> 00:11:17,412
Vera was the expert.

176
00:11:17,452 --> 00:11:19,606
She had done eleven spacewalks.

177
00:11:19,646 --> 00:11:23,498
But nobody can work in a spacesuit with a broken wrist.

178
00:11:23,538 --> 00:11:26,590
There was only one other person on board.

179
00:11:27,690 --> 00:11:30,907
I'll be in your ear the whole time. Every bolt, every move.

180
00:11:30,947 --> 00:11:33,636
You trained for this, Noah. Remember the pool.

181
00:11:34,736 --> 00:11:38,139
In the pool, if I let go, I just floated to the bottom.

182
00:11:39,239 --> 00:11:42,667
It took four hours to put on the suit and prepare.

183
00:11:42,707 --> 00:11:44,609
Then the outer hatch opened.

184
00:11:44,649 --> 00:11:47,712
And Noah pulled himself out into the void.

185
00:11:48,812 --> 00:11:52,096
There is no up or down outside a space station.

186
00:11:52,136 --> 00:11:56,126
Only the Earth, filling half of everything. Huge. Bright.

187
00:11:56,166 --> 00:11:58,601
Turning silently beneath his boots.

188
00:11:58,641 --> 00:12:01,712
And a thin steel cable, clipped to his belt,

189
00:12:01,752 --> 00:12:05,721
the only thing connecting him to the rest of humanity.

190
00:12:06,821 --> 00:12:11,025
Breathe, Noah. Slowly. One hand, then the other. Never let go with both.

191
00:12:12,125 --> 00:12:17,625
He crawled along the outside of the station, hand over hand, toward the antenna.

192
00:12:17,665 --> 00:12:20,949
It took him fifty minutes to move twenty meters.

193
00:12:20,989 --> 00:12:23,718
He unbolted the antenna, piece by piece,

194
00:12:23,758 --> 00:12:27,440
while sweat floated off his face inside his helmet.

195
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:31,275
And then the sun went out. Orbital night.

196
00:12:31,315 --> 00:12:36,621
In less than ten seconds, the world went from blinding white to absolute black.

197
00:12:36,661 --> 00:12:40,343
Noah was alone in the dark, with only his helmet light,

198
00:12:40,383 --> 00:12:43,308
and the cities of Asia glowing far below.

199
00:12:44,408 --> 00:12:49,832
As he turned to move back, his tether snagged on a broken edge of the solar panel.

200
00:12:49,871 --> 00:12:52,763
He pulled. It didn't move. He pulled harder.

201
00:12:52,803 --> 00:12:55,485
And the hook on his belt slipped open.

202
00:12:56,585 --> 00:12:59,507
Noah! Grab the rail! The rail on your left! Now!

203
00:13:00,607 --> 00:13:05,875
His glove closed around the metal rail with nothing but a few centimeters to spare.

204
00:13:05,915 --> 00:13:09,008
For a long moment, he just hung there, breathing.

205
00:13:09,048 --> 00:13:13,994
Between him and the endless black, there had been one second, and one hand.

206
00:13:15,094 --> 00:13:16,295
He clipped back in.

207
00:13:16,335 --> 00:13:19,168
He carried the antenna to the living module.

208
00:13:19,208 --> 00:13:22,368
He connected the cable, exactly as Vera told him.

209
00:13:22,408 --> 00:13:25,698
And when he finally floated back through the hatch,

210
00:13:25,738 --> 00:13:30,269
his legs were shaking so much he couldn't stand. Not that it mattered.

211
00:13:30,309 --> 00:13:32,418
There was nothing to stand on.

212
00:13:33,518 --> 00:13:38,475
Vera switched on the transmitter. A green light blinked. And then it died.

213
00:13:38,515 --> 00:13:43,674
The fragment that had cut the main antenna had also burned out the amplifier.

214
00:13:43,714 --> 00:13:45,295
The antenna was perfect.

215
00:13:45,335 --> 00:13:48,118
But there was nothing left to power it.

216
00:13:53,218 --> 00:13:55,486
That night, neither of them slept.

217
00:13:55,526 --> 00:13:58,134
The oxygen gauge showed four days left.

218
00:13:58,174 --> 00:14:04,027
The nearest rescue was a world away, and the world didn't even know they needed one.

219
00:14:05,127 --> 00:14:10,624
Noah was floating near the storage lockers, unable to rest, when he saw it.

220
00:14:10,664 --> 00:14:16,055
A small yellow box with a label in Vera's handwriting. School contacts.

221
00:14:17,155 --> 00:14:18,523
The ham radio! I forgot.

222
00:14:18,563 --> 00:14:20,341
We brought it for the children.

223
00:14:20,381 --> 00:14:24,153
Schools around the world call the station with homemade antennas,

224
00:14:24,193 --> 00:14:28,096
and ask us questions. Do you sleep standing up. What do you eat.

225
00:14:29,196 --> 00:14:31,083
It was a tiny amateur radio.

226
00:14:31,123 --> 00:14:33,492
Less powerful than a walkie-talkie.

227
00:14:33,532 --> 00:14:35,970
It didn't need the broken amplifier.

228
00:14:36,010 --> 00:14:38,104
But it had one huge limitation.

229
00:14:38,144 --> 00:14:42,027
It could only reach someone almost directly beneath them,

230
00:14:42,067 --> 00:14:45,727
during the ten minutes the station passed overhead.

231
00:14:46,827 --> 00:14:50,725
And most of the Earth is ocean. Most of the land is empty.

232
00:14:50,765 --> 00:14:55,056
Most of the time, there would be nobody below them listening.

233
00:14:56,156 --> 00:15:01,310
Then we call every pass. Every ninety-two minutes. Until someone answers.

234
00:15:02,410 --> 00:15:05,908
And so they did. Over the Pacific. Over Siberia.

235
00:15:05,948 --> 00:15:11,363
Over the Sahara, where Noah thought of Idris and his three glasses of tea.

236
00:15:11,403 --> 00:15:15,018
Over and over, the same words, into the static.

237
00:15:16,118 --> 00:15:19,832
CQ, CQ, this is space station Meridian. Emergency.

238
00:15:19,872 --> 00:15:23,325
Anyone who can hear us, please answer. Over.

239
00:15:24,425 --> 00:15:28,715
Sixteen passes. Twenty. Twenty-five. Nothing but static.

240
00:15:28,755 --> 00:15:33,818
The carbon dioxide was rising again, as the last filters wore out.

241
00:15:33,858 --> 00:15:35,751
Noah's head was pounding.

242
00:15:35,791 --> 00:15:38,415
Vera's voice was getting slower.

243
00:15:39,515 --> 00:15:42,677
On the second night, after the thirty-first call,

244
00:15:42,717 --> 00:15:47,577
Noah stopped speaking in the middle of the message. He just held the radio.

245
00:15:47,617 --> 00:15:52,210
And watched the dark curve of South America sliding past the window.

246
00:15:57,310 --> 00:16:02,023
Twelve thousand kilometers away, and four hundred kilometers below,

247
00:16:02,063 --> 00:16:05,996
in a village at the edge of the Atacama Desert in Chile,

248
00:16:06,036 --> 00:16:11,365
a fifteen-year-old girl named Sofía was sitting on the roof of her house.

249
00:16:12,465 --> 00:16:15,305
The Atacama is the driest place on Earth.

250
00:16:15,345 --> 00:16:17,412
And it has the clearest skies.

251
00:16:17,452 --> 00:16:22,398
Sofía's grandfather had been an amateur radio operator for fifty years.

252
00:16:22,438 --> 00:16:27,876
Every clear night, they used to sit on this roof together, with his old radio,

253
00:16:27,916 --> 00:16:31,578
and listen to the space stations passing overhead.

254
00:16:32,678 --> 00:16:34,529
He had died the year before.

255
00:16:34,569 --> 00:16:38,515
Sofía still came up to the roof. Still turned on the radio.

256
00:16:38,555 --> 00:16:43,839
Mostly, she said, so that he wouldn't be the only one who stopped listening.

257
00:16:44,939 --> 00:16:49,955
That night, through the hiss of the speaker, she heard a voice. Broken. Tired.

258
00:16:49,995 --> 00:16:51,442
Speaking in English.

259
00:16:52,542 --> 00:16:55,908
...Meridian... emergency... anyone who can hear us...

260
00:16:57,008 --> 00:17:01,375
Meridian, Meridian! I hear you! This is Sofía, in Chile! I hear you! Over!

261
00:17:02,475 --> 00:17:07,119
On the station, Noah almost dropped the radio. Vera grabbed his arm.

262
00:17:07,159 --> 00:17:12,010
They had eight minutes before the station disappeared over the horizon.

263
00:17:12,050 --> 00:17:16,747
Eight minutes to tell the entire story to a teenager on a rooftop.

264
00:17:17,847 --> 00:17:23,208
Sofía, listen to me. Write this down. Two crew, alive. Antenna destroyed.

265
00:17:23,248 --> 00:17:26,981
Capsule heat shield damaged. Oxygen for three days.

266
00:17:27,021 --> 00:17:29,200
Please, call the space agency.

267
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:31,462
Tell them Meridian is alive.

268
00:17:32,562 --> 00:17:36,852
Sofía wrote every word on the back of her chemistry homework.

269
00:17:36,892 --> 00:17:39,195
Then the voice faded into static,

270
00:17:39,235 --> 00:17:43,644
and the little moving star disappeared behind the mountains.

271
00:17:44,744 --> 00:17:48,956
She called the police. They laughed. She called the radio club.

272
00:17:48,996 --> 00:17:51,520
Nobody answered at two in the morning.

273
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:55,637
So she put on her jacket, took her grandfather's old bicycle,

274
00:17:55,677 --> 00:17:59,349
and rode eleven kilometers in the dark, up a dirt road,

275
00:17:59,389 --> 00:18:03,048
to the big astronomical observatory on the mountain.

276
00:18:04,148 --> 00:18:07,176
The guard at the gate didn't want to let her in.

277
00:18:07,216 --> 00:18:11,714
But an astronomer working the night shift heard the shouting, came out,

278
00:18:11,754 --> 00:18:13,439
and read the homework page.

279
00:18:13,479 --> 00:18:16,891
And then she looked at the orbit tracker on her phone.

280
00:18:16,931 --> 00:18:22,066
Meridian had passed directly over this valley, at exactly the time Sofía said.

281
00:18:23,166 --> 00:18:27,640
Within an hour, the observatory was on the phone with mission control.

282
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:30,026
And by sunrise, the whole world knew.

283
00:18:30,066 --> 00:18:32,412
Two people were still alive up there.

284
00:18:32,452 --> 00:18:36,471
And a fifteen-year-old girl had been the only one listening.

285
00:18:37,571 --> 00:18:40,607
Meridian, Control. We hear you. We hear you. Welcome back.

286
00:18:40,647 --> 00:18:43,660
A rescue capsule is being prepared right now. Hang on.

287
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:53,514
The good news was that a rescue capsule could launch in three days.

288
00:18:53,554 --> 00:18:55,518
The bad news was the oxygen.

289
00:18:55,558 --> 00:18:58,570
By Vera's math, they had two and a half.

290
00:18:59,670 --> 00:19:01,367
Control sent them a plan.

291
00:19:01,407 --> 00:19:06,855
Every ground station on Earth with an amateur radio was now listening for them.

292
00:19:06,895 --> 00:19:11,024
The plan was simple, and brutal. Move as little as possible.

293
00:19:11,064 --> 00:19:16,494
Talk as little as possible. Sleep as much as possible. Every breath counted.

294
00:19:17,594 --> 00:19:22,406
So they lay still, in the dim light, strapped into their sleeping bags,

295
00:19:22,446 --> 00:19:23,295
side by side.

296
00:19:23,335 --> 00:19:27,122
And during the long hours, Vera talked. Slowly. Quietly.

297
00:19:27,162 --> 00:19:32,232
About her daughter, who was nineteen and thought astronauts were boring.

298
00:19:33,332 --> 00:19:37,169
On my first mission I was so busy I never looked out the window.

299
00:19:37,209 --> 00:19:40,259
Do you know what I regret the most? Not the danger.

300
00:19:40,299 --> 00:19:42,145
The sunrises I didn't watch.

301
00:19:43,245 --> 00:19:45,687
So Noah woke her up for the next one.

302
00:19:45,727 --> 00:19:48,974
They floated to the cupola together, very slowly,

303
00:19:49,014 --> 00:19:52,596
and watched the line of fire sweep across the Pacific.

304
00:19:52,636 --> 00:19:55,804
Sunrise number one hundred and seventy-three.

305
00:19:56,904 --> 00:20:00,574
On the last day, the oxygen alarm began to beep softly.

306
00:20:00,614 --> 00:20:03,137
Noah's vision was blurry at the edges.

307
00:20:03,177 --> 00:20:05,903
Every thought took a long time to arrive.

308
00:20:05,943 --> 00:20:10,275
He took a photograph of the Earth, and could not remember why.

309
00:20:11,375 --> 00:20:16,666
Then, through the window, he saw a new light. Small and white. Getting bigger.

310
00:20:16,706 --> 00:20:17,691
Getting closer.

311
00:20:17,731 --> 00:20:22,323
Firing tiny bursts of gas as it lined itself up with the station.

312
00:20:23,423 --> 00:20:27,866
Meridian, this is Dawn. We are thirty meters out. Coming to you. Put the kettle on.

313
00:20:28,966 --> 00:20:31,530
The docking clamps closed with a clang.

314
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:35,871
And when the hatch opened, the first thing Noah felt was the air.

315
00:20:35,911 --> 00:20:40,546
Cold, clean, fresh air from the rescue ship, pouring into the station.

316
00:20:40,586 --> 00:20:43,607
He breathed it in like water in the desert.

317
00:20:48,707 --> 00:20:54,175
Twelve hours later, the rescue capsule undocked, carrying four people home.

318
00:20:54,215 --> 00:20:59,315
Behind them, Meridian grew smaller, its golden wings catching the sun.

319
00:20:59,355 --> 00:21:00,607
Empty, for now.

320
00:21:01,707 --> 00:21:05,611
Coming home from space is like being thrown into a furnace.

321
00:21:05,651 --> 00:21:10,691
The capsule hit the atmosphere at twenty-eight thousand kilometers per hour,

322
00:21:10,731 --> 00:21:13,432
and the window turned orange, then white,

323
00:21:13,472 --> 00:21:16,897
as a cloud of burning plasma wrapped around them.

324
00:21:17,997 --> 00:21:19,790
Then a jolt. Then another.

325
00:21:19,830 --> 00:21:23,175
Four huge parachutes bloomed against a blue sky.

326
00:21:23,215 --> 00:21:27,195
And for the first time in eleven days, Noah felt gravity.

327
00:21:27,235 --> 00:21:31,686
Heavy, beautiful gravity, pulling him back toward the ground.

328
00:21:32,786 --> 00:21:35,965
They splashed down in the Pacific Ocean at dawn.

329
00:21:36,005 --> 00:21:37,842
Noah laughed until he cried.

330
00:21:37,882 --> 00:21:43,140
After everything, he had come home exactly where he swore he never wanted to be

331
00:21:43,180 --> 00:21:45,476
again. In the middle of the sea.

332
00:21:46,576 --> 00:21:48,764
Vera needed surgery on her wrist.

333
00:21:48,804 --> 00:21:53,219
Noah needed three weeks to learn how to walk without falling over.

334
00:21:53,259 --> 00:21:58,944
And the first thing he did, when his legs worked again, was buy a ticket to Chile.

335
00:22:00,044 --> 00:22:05,110
Sofía was waiting for him on the same roof, with her grandfather's radio.

336
00:22:05,150 --> 00:22:08,328
She was taller than he expected, and very shy.

337
00:22:08,368 --> 00:22:10,686
Until he gave her a photograph.

338
00:22:11,786 --> 00:22:16,589
He had taken it from the cupola, on the night of the thirty-second call.

339
00:22:16,629 --> 00:22:19,279
The dark curve of the Andes. The desert.

340
00:22:19,319 --> 00:22:24,862
And in the middle of all that blackness, one tiny point of light. A single village.

341
00:22:24,902 --> 00:22:25,859
Her village.

342
00:22:26,959 --> 00:22:27,755
That's my house.

343
00:22:27,795 --> 00:22:30,192
You took a picture of my house. From space.

344
00:22:31,292 --> 00:22:34,106
Today, the space company has a new rule.

345
00:22:34,146 --> 00:22:39,387
Every station, every capsule, carries a small amateur radio. Just in case.

346
00:22:39,427 --> 00:22:45,024
And on the frame of the cupola window, someone has written two words in marker.

347
00:22:45,064 --> 00:22:46,284
Keep listening.

348
00:22:47,384 --> 00:22:50,756
Every few months, Noah gets a message from Chile.

349
00:22:50,796 --> 00:22:56,725
A photograph of the night sky, with a long bright streak across it. And four words.

350
00:22:57,825 --> 00:23:00,885
Because sometimes, when everything else fails,

351
00:23:00,925 --> 00:23:05,399
what saves us is not the most powerful machine. It's a small voice.

352
00:23:05,439 --> 00:23:09,093
And someone, somewhere, who never stopped listening.

353
00:23:15,193 --> 00:23:18,668
If you were stranded in space with three days of air,

354
00:23:18,708 --> 00:23:20,724
and a broken antenna outside...

355
00:23:20,764 --> 00:23:26,163
would you climb out into the void, with one thin cable holding you to the station?

356
00:23:26,203 --> 00:23:27,945
Tell us in the comments.

357
00:23:29,045 --> 00:23:30,089
And remember...

358
00:23:30,129 --> 00:23:33,097
when everything ends, the story begins.
